{number: 33}
{title: When I Survey the Wondrous Cross}
{key: F}

{start_of_verse: 1}
When [F] I survey the [Bb] wondrous [F] cross
On which the [Dm] Prince of [C] glory [F] died,
My richest gain I [Bb] count but [F] loss,
And pour con-[Dm] tempt on [C7] all my [F] pride.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: 2}
For-[F] bid it, Lord, that [Bb] I should [F] boast,
Save in the [Dm] cross of [C] Christ, my [F] God:
All the vain things that [Bb] charm me [F] most,
I sacrifice [Dm] them to [C7] His [F] blood.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: 3}
[F] See, from His head, His [Bb] hands, His [F] feet,
Sorrow and [Dm] love flow [C] mingled [F] down!
Did e'er such love and [Bb] sorrow [F] meet?
Or thorns com-[Dm] pose so [C7] rich a [F] crown?
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: 4}
Were [F] the whole realm of [Bb] nature [F] mine,
That were a [Dm] tribute [C] far too [F] small;
Love so amazing, [Bb] so di-[F] vine,
Demands my [Dm] soul, my [C7] life, my [F] all.
{end_of_verse}
